/**
\page profiles_lane iccApplyProfiles: TIFF profile application lane

\tableofcontents

\section profiles_contract Input forms and argv ownership

This tool converts a source TIFF to a destination TIFF through a standard
profile sequence.

| Form | Token shape |
|---|---|
| JSON | `iccApplyProfiles [--threads N] [--ifd N] --cfg config_file` |
| Direct | `iccApplyProfiles [--threads N] [--ifd N] [--exportcfg config_file] src_tiff dst_tiff encoding compression planar embed interpolation profile intent ...` |

Global options are parsed before either form. `--threads N` accepts 0 through
`CIccThreadedCmm::GetMaxThreads()` (currently 256); zero requests hardware
concurrency and one requests a single worker. `--ifd N` accepts 0 through
4095 and overrides `imageFiles.srcIfd` in a JSON configuration. The tool
advances its local `argv` and reduces `argc` for consumed options, so
`--cfg file` must be the exactly three remaining tokens: program name,
`--cfg`, and file. Legacy single-dash forms remain accepted where provided by
`Usage()`.

\dotfile profiles-lane.dot "iccApplyProfiles parser and TIFF transform lane"

The direct form first removes the program name, then optionally consumes
`-exportcfg file`. `CIccCfgImageApply::fromArgs()` owns the fixed TIFF output
group; `CIccCfgProfileSequence::fromArgs()` owns the remaining profile group.
The final consumed count must equal the offered profile remainder. An odd
trailing direct-mode token is rejected rather than ignored.

\section profiles_options Direct configuration group

| Field | Accepted selector | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `src_tiff`, `dst_tiff` | paths | Input and destination TIFF files. |
| `--ifd N`, `imageFiles.srcIfd` | main-chain TIFF directory 0 through 4095 | Select one source IFD; CLI selection overrides JSON and both default to 0. |
| `encoding` | 0, 1, 2, 3 | Preserve source, 8-bit, 16-bit, or float output samples. |
| `compression` | 0, 1 | No compression or LZW compression. |
| `planar` | 0, 1 | Contiguous or separate TIFF planes. |
| `embed` | 0, 1 | Do not embed an ICC profile, or embed the final ICC profile. |
| `interpolation` | 0, 1 | Linear or tetrahedral interpolation. |
| profile sequence | pairs with optional `-ENV:sig value` and `-PCC file` | Standard CMM transforms and connection conditions. |

The rendering-intent selector supports the documented base, transform,
preview, gamut, BPC, BRDF, and MCS values printed by `Usage()`. Add 1000 for
luminance-based PCS adjustment and 10000 to use a V5 sub-profile where present.

\section profiles_search JSON inverse-search chains (connect.useSearch)

Setting `useSearch` to `true` inside the JSON `connect` block builds a
CIccCmmSearch instead of the forward CIccCmm, so an image can be reproduced
through a spectral inverse search. It is `-cfg` only: the direct form has no
room for the weighted PCC list or the reverse-search start, and `-exportcfg`
output is unchanged.

| Key | Location | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `useSearch` | `connect` | Build a CIccCmmSearch from `profileSequence`. Emitted by `toJson()` only when true, so existing configs round-trip byte-identically. |
| `threads` | `connect` | Applies to a search chain as it does to a forward one. Each worker holds its own CIccApplyCmmSearch with private sub-chain apply objects, so a threaded result matches the scalar one. |
| `searchApply` | top level | The search chain: `profileSequence`, `initial` and `pccWeights` in one object, spelled exactly as iccApplySearch spells it, so the block moves between the two tools verbatim. |
| `pccWeights` | in `searchApply` | Weighted profile connection conditions the search optimises across. Required when `profileSequence` has three entries. |
| `initial` | in `searchApply` | Optional initial-destination settings seeding the reverse search. Absent means the destination profile seeds it. |

Under `useSearch` the chain is read from `searchApply`; a top-level
`profileSequence` is the forward-mode spelling and is not consulted, so the
chain is only ever written in one place. Naming a `searchApply` block with
`useSearch` off is an error rather than a silent forward run. The schema
(`docs/icc-connect-config.schema.json`) states the pair directly: the accepted
top-level shapes include `imageFiles` + `profileSequence` for a forward chain
and `imageFiles` + `searchApply` for a search. Rules the tool enforces before
building the CMM:

- Two or three profiles. CIccCmmSearch accepts a source, an optional mid, and a
  destination; anything else is rejected by name.
- Three profiles require at least one `pccWeights` entry, since the mid and
  destination connect through the weighted PCC set.
- With `useSearch` off, naming `initial` or `pccWeights` is an error rather than
  a silent no-op.

An empty first `iccFile` still selects the source image's embedded profile, and
`useV5SubProfile` on that entry reaches a v5 multispectral sub-profile. That is
the only way to drive an image whose colour description travels inside it, such
as the 8-channel multispectral data in `Testing/hybrid`. A per-stage `pccFile`
is discarded by the search CMM everywhere except the last entry, which supplies
the initial-destination chain; the tool warns on stderr where it is inert.

A search apply is orders of magnitude slower per pixel than a forward chain,
because every pixel runs a Nelder-Mead search rather than a fixed sequence of
xforms.

\section profiles_runtime Transform and output behavior

Before decoding, the tool inventories the bounded main IFD chain and selects
the requested directory. It warns when other main IFDs or selected-directory
SubIFDs are present; the destination contains only the transformed selected
main IFD. SubIFDs are counted but never traversed. Telemetry-enabled builds
record the selected index, whether it came from the default, JSON, or CLI,
and the discovered main-IFD and selected-SubIFD counts.

The tool then opens the selected source IFD, accepts 8-bit, 16-bit, or 32-bit
float samples, derives or validates the destination bit depth, and obtains any
embedded source profile. An empty first profile file path requests that
embedded profile. It calls `CIccConnectCmm::CreateStandard()` for a forward
chain or `CreateSearch()` when JSON `connect.useSearch` is true.

For every image row and pixel group, the tool converts source samples to
internal representation, applies the CMM, converts to the chosen destination
representation, and writes the configured TIFF layout.

\section profiles_qa QA example

`iccApplyProfiles-quick-check.sh` verifies each output encoding selector,
JSON export/replay, exact `-cfg` and direct-argument rejection, and a
single-threaded path. It uses checked-in TIFF and ICC fixtures.

\ref md_docs_2iccapply_2apply-profile-processing "ApplyProfiles processing QA"
defines the output and evidence contract. \ref tiff_processing_pipeline
"TIFF image processing pipeline" shows how this transform relates to
SpecSepToTiff and TiffDump.
*/
